
It more or less followed the steps of the software development cycle.

\subsubsection{Analysis}

The need was a "krita palette" for trame-slicer. Lucie, Thibault and Julien were
the product owners.

The krita palette has a big variety of features and shows as a radial buttons
around the mouse click \cref{fig:krita-palette}. It has a side menu to the right
for the brush options, and it makes it easy for the user to quickly switch
between different drawing tools. Trame slicer's medical viewer can benefit from
the same interface.

\insertfigure
    {assets/images/krita-palette}
    {height=0.3\textheight}
    {Krita palette \cite{krita}}
    {fig:krita-palette}

Trame slicer's medical viewer almost has the same tools and right now these
tools are in a side menu as shown in \cref{fig:medical-viewer-ui}

\insertfigure
    {assets/images/medical-viewer-ui}
    {height=0.3\textheight}
    {Medical Viewer UI}
    {fig:medical-viewer-ui}

First step was to establish a state of the art \cref{appendix:sota-pie-menu}.

It was about whether some solution already exists (and we want it), features,
design, technologies used, licence.

No item of the state of the art fitted the expectations, it was decided to
develop our own radial menu, with design choices and technology choices inspired
from the items of the state of the art.

\subsubsection{Development}

Then was the development of the radial menu in VueJS. A first POC
\cref{fig:first-poc-radial-menu} was shown to the product owners, their feedback
refined the design, the API and the features they wanted.

\insertfigure
    {assets/images/radial-menu-first-poc}
